<h2 id="section-1">Begin with the wearing task</h2>
<p>Describe the object and its role: a pendant with your usual shirts, a ring intended for a particular finger, or earrings for occasional events. Include activities and equipment that affect the piece, without sharing unnecessary private details. “I want a sculptural outline that does not meet my usual collar” is more actionable than “something unique and comfortable.” The maker can then ask about dimensions, construction and a fitting route.</p>
<p>List what you already own if it must connect to the new piece. Supply exact references or ask how an older object should be identified. Do not assume that specifying an opening measurement resolves compatibility. If you are providing an heirloom or stone, request a professional assessment before building a design around an unverified material or an assumed condition.</p>
<h2 id="section-4">Use this one-page brief</h2>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Field</th><th scope="col">What to write</th><th scope="col">What to leave for confirmation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intended use</td><td>Position, ordinary activities and desired frequency</td><td>Actual suitability and construction.</td></tr><tr><td>Visual priorities</td><td>Shape, color relationship and preferred scale</td><td>Final dimensions and surface process.</td></tr><tr><td>Must preserve</td><td>A mark, object, stone or recognizable feature</td><td>Feasibility and any irreversible change.</td></tr><tr><td>Fit reference</td><td>A comfortable existing piece and known measurements</td><td>New fit, tolerances and fitting method.</td></tr><tr><td>Budget and date</td><td>Comfortable total and the real occasion</td><td>Quote, milestones and delivery commitment.</td></tr><tr><td>Approval</td><td>Who decides and which questions remain</td><td>What approval authorizes at each stage.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Mark priorities as essential, preferred or open. This helps when two preferences compete. A dramatic profile, a very small scale and an intricate detail may need a discussion rather than a promise that everything is achievable. Ask the maker to explain proposed compromises in language you understand. A production constraint is useful information, not an invitation to guess at engineering yourself.</p>
<h2 id="section-7">Use references to communicate, not duplicate</h2>
<p>For each reference image, say what you like: the proportion, negative space, texture direction or relation of colors. Use several distinct references when helpful and include a note about what you do not want. A moodboard is easier to interpret when every image has a purpose. Do not ask the maker to reproduce another brand’s identifiable design or assume an image found online is free to copy.</p>
<p>If a symbol or inscription matters, provide the exact text and orientation yourself, then ask how it will be shown for approval. Check spelling, language and placement separately from the overall silhouette. A correct front view does not settle what appears on the back, which surface faces the body, or whether a mark remains visible in the intended position.</p>
<h2 id="section-10">Agree the process before refining the picture</h2>
<p>Ask which stages are offered: sketch, dimensioned drawing, digital model, physical sample, fitting and final inspection. Not every commission includes all of them. Establish what each stage can demonstrate and what it cannot. A rendering can communicate appearance; it is not evidence of the final material, weight or fit. Ask how a change after approval is handled, quoted and recorded.</p>
<p>In a fictional project, the client initially asks for “a bold ring with my family symbol.” The brief reveals that preserving the handwritten form matters more than adding a stone, and that the ring must suit a familiar daily activity. That information changes the conversation before a detailed image is produced. The maker can propose a route and explain what needs testing, instead of polishing an attractive but poorly defined concept.</p>
<h2 id="section-13">Keep a decision log</h2>
<p>After each discussion, record the selected version, remaining questions, cost implications and next authorized step. Keep one current brief rather than several contradictory messages. If more than one person is commissioning the piece, name who gives final approval. A maker should not have to infer the final choice from reactions to different images in a long conversation.</p>
<p>SUIJI’s vision includes jewelry with a strong personal character and designs shaped by their wearer. That vision is not a promise that every custom project is currently available to order. For a present request, ask about the actual service, supported products and process. This brief is useful whether you speak to SUIJI, another maker or decide a ready-made piece already meets the need.</p>
<h2 id="section-16">A completed miniature brief</h2>
<p>Consider this fictional starting point: ‘I want a pendant with a clear oval outline for my usual open-collar shirts. I prefer one visible motif rather than several hanging objects. The outline matters more than adding a stone. I would like the connection explained and shown, including its back. My budget is a total limit, not just a price for the decorative front. The date is preferred rather than essential.’ This gives a maker several questions to answer without pretending to specify a finished object.</p>
<p>The next version of the brief should contain the maker’s answers, not simply repeat the client’s first wishes. If a proposed dimension changes the effect, record the new choice. If a different construction is needed, ask how the appearance and operation change. Replace unclear statements with confirmed ones while preserving the original priorities. That creates a document both people can use instead of a collection of attractive but contradictory references.</p>
<h2 id="section-19">What to send and what to keep private</h2>
<p>Send a clean reference image of an existing piece only when it helps explain the task. Crop out unrelated addresses, receipts with payment details or private family messages. If an inscription contains personal information, ask how it will be handled and whether it will appear in public portfolio images. Permission to make an object is a separate question from permission to publish its story or your likeness. You can ask about the maker’s actual process without assuming a particular privacy service is offered.</p>
<p>Before sending a valuable object, ask about the agreed handover route, identification and insurance arrangements. A first design conversation does not require an unplanned shipment. Keep the physical-object decision separate from sharing an idea.</p>
<h2 id="section-22">The decision to take away</h2>
<p>Your completed brief should let another person explain the intended object, the most important features and the unresolved questions in a few sentences. If they cannot, simplify it before requesting a polished render. A useful next step might be a clarification, a feasibility discussion or a comparison with an existing piece. It does not have to be immediate production. This is how a personal idea becomes a reviewable project without asking either person to guess what the other means.</p>
<h2 id="section-24">Before you send the brief</h2>
<p>Read the document once as the wearer and once as someone who has never seen your references. The wearer should recognize the intended role. The reader should find the object, priorities, budget boundary and next question without opening a long message history. Remove conflicting references or explain why they are there. Identify anything supplied by you, anything the maker must source and anything still unknown. Finally, state the next request clearly: a conversation, a feasibility assessment, a quote or a specified design stage. This makes the brief usable before anyone invests in a polished image.</p>
<h2 id="section-26">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="section-27">Do I need to draw well?</h3>
<p>No. A clear description, reference annotations and priorities can begin the conversation. Ask what format the maker needs rather than presenting a polished picture as a production specification.</p>
<h3 id="section-29">Should I specify every technical dimension?</h3>
<p>Provide known fit references and important constraints, but ask the maker to establish feasible dimensions and tolerances. Invented precision can create confusion instead of clarity.</p>
<h3 id="section-31">Can the brief guarantee the quote?</h3>
<p>No. It helps define the task. The maker must confirm scope, materials, process and price, including what happens if the project changes.</p>
<h3 id="section-33">What if a ready-made piece fits the brief?</h3>
<p>That is a useful discovery. Personal meaning does not require a custom commission, and the brief can help you recognize an existing piece that already serves your role.</p>
